WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:03.920
Here's a scenario every respiratory
therapist encounters.

2
00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:09.479
A patient with a perfectly normal ABG with
normal PaO2 and normal saturation.

3
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.060
And yet, they're in tissue hypoxia.

4
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.119
The cells are suffocating even though the
blood looks fine.

5
00:00:15.859 --> 00:00:15.960
How?

6
00:00:16.119 --> 00:00:19.020
Because hypoxemia and hypoxia are not the
same thing.

7
00:00:19.819 --> 00:00:24.340
This lesson draws that line clearly and
gives you the framework to assess both.

8
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.059
Hypoxemia is a laboratory finding if the
PaO2 is below 80 mmHg.

9
00:00:30.059 --> 00:00:33.319
It's measurable, objective, and classified
from

10
00:00:33.319 --> 00:00:33.979
mild to severe.

11
00:00:34.599 --> 00:00:35.819
We cover all five causes.

12
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:39.400
Hypoventilation, V/Q mismatch, diffusion
impairment,

13
00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:41.419
shunting, and low-inspired oxygen.

14
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.040
Hypoxia is a cellular state.

15
00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:45.159
There are four types.

16
00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:49.240
Hypoxemic hypoxia, where the lungs fail to
load oxygen onto

17
00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:49.779
hemoglobin.

18
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:54.479
Ischemic hypoxia, where the heart fails to
deliver oxygenated blood.

19
00:00:55.099 --> 00:00:58.419
Anemic hypoxia, where there's simply not
enough hemoglobin to carry

20
00:00:58.419 --> 00:00:59.560
what the lungs provide.

21
00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:04.380
And histotoxic hypoxia, where cyanide or
another toxin prevents cells

22
00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:06.639
from using the oxygen that's everywhere
around them.

23
00:01:07.419 --> 00:01:08.959
This distinction changes treatment.

24
00:01:09.839 --> 00:01:11.480
Supplemental oxygen helps one type.

25
00:01:11.959 --> 00:01:13.559
For the others, it's the wrong answer.

26
00:01:14.099 --> 00:01:17.300
And giving oxygen instead of the right
treatment wastes critical time.

27
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:22.480
We also cover the CaO2 and DO2 formulas,
the oxyhemoglobin

28
00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:26.000
dissociation curve and its clinical
shifts, the A-a gradient as

29
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.599
a locator for where the problem lives, and
the clinical

30
00:01:28.599 --> 00:01:31.959
signs that move from early RAT signs to
late BED-C signs.

31
00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.980
Normal ABG does not rule out hypoxia.

32
00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:38.019
This lesson will show you why and what to
do about it.

33
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:39.559
Let's get started.

